<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chlothar I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 497–561</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Clotaire I, Chlothar the Old · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merovingian-dynasty">Merovingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chlothar I was a Merovingian king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a>. On his father's death in 511 he received a share of the kingdom around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a>, and with his brothers he conquered the Burgundian kingdom in 534 and took part in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. Outliving his brothers and nephews, some of whom he murdered, he reunited the Frankish realm from 558, which was divided again among his sons in 561.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chlothar-i.jpg" alt="Chlothar I" width="320" height="149" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monnaie_d%27argent_de_Clotaire_Ier.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…family inheritance, dividing it among their sons, so that it was repeatedly split into kingdoms that came to be known as Austrasia in the east, Neustria in the west and Burgundy, and reunited when lines died out, as under <b>Chlothar I</b> in 558 and Chlothar II in 613.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>They were long a match for the Franks: in the 550s they inflicted a heavy defeat on <b>Chlothar I</b>, and in the 8th century they resisted repeated campaigns by Charles Martel and Pippin the Short.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Childebert died after a long illness without heirs, allowing his brother <b>Chlothar I</b> to reunite the Frankish kingdom, as their father Clovis I had done, and to seize Childebert's treasure.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At the division of 561 <b>Chlothar I's</b> son Guntram received Burgundy with Orléans, ruling from Chalon-sur-Saône.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-burgundy">Frankish Kingdom of Burgundy</a></li><li><blockquote>When Clovis died in 511, his youngest son <b>Chlothar I</b> received the old Frankish heartland in the north, between the Somme and the Scheldt, ruling from Soissons, the former seat of the Domain of Soissons.</blockquote><a href="/state/frankish-kingdom-of-soissons">Frankish Kingdom of Soissons</a></li><li><blockquote>His uncles Childebert I and <b>Chlothar I</b> murdered two of his young sons and divided his kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-orleans">Kingdom of Orléans</a></li></ul></section>
